```
The crush detection is in checkCurrentPlane at lines 2476-2524. Here's how it works:

  Trigger condition: The first wall check (radius = unk15C, up to 2 results) found exactly 2 walls.

  Step 1 — Are the walls facing each other?
  f32 dot = wall0->mNormal.y * wall1->mNormal.y
          + wall0->mNormal.x * wall1->mNormal.x
          + wall0->mNormal.z * wall1->mNormal.z;
  if (dot < -0.9f) { ... }
  It computes the dot product of the two wall normals. A dot product of -1.0 means perfectly opposing faces; the
  threshold of -0.9 means the walls must be nearly parallel and facing each other (within ~25 degrees). Two walls at a
  90-degree corner (dot = 0) won't trigger this.

  Step 2 — Is either wall owned by a specific actor type?
  u32 actorType = *(u32*)((u8*)actor0 + 0x4C);
  if ((actorType - 0x40000000) == 0x2BD)
      doCrush = 1;
  It checks if either wall's owning actor has type 0x400002BD. This prevents crush damage from static geometry — it only
   fires when at least one wall belongs to a moving object (likely a press/crusher/sliding block). You can't get crushed
   between two static walls.

  Step 3 — Is Mario actually squeezed?
  f32 dist0 = wall0->normal · pos + wall0->planeDistance;
  f32 dist1 = wall1->normal · pos + wall1->planeDistance;
  if (dist0 < 10.0f || dist1 < 10.0f) {
      floorDamageExec(...);
  }
  It computes the signed distance from Mario's position to each wall plane. If Mario is within 10 units of either wall,
  he takes damage via floorDamageExec with damage type 3 (knockdown). The damage amount comes from this + 0x58C and
  invincibility time from this + 0x27BC.

  In summary: Two opposing walls (dot < -0.9) + at least one is a moving actor (0x2BD) + Mario within 10 units of either
   = crush damage. 
```

